 RBOPS dispatches. It is injected verbatim into every phase context by `rbops/dispatch.sh`. It is law, not advice.  You are running inside an automated pipeline. There is no human in your loop. The only thing that makes your work count is that it passes a machine gate. Nothing you *say* about your work is evidence. Only what `rbops/verify.sh` proves is evidence.  ---  ## 0. What RBOPS is  RBOPS is a self-dispatching, self-auditing development pipeline for the **Redblue** programming language.  - **Dispatcher** (`rbops/dispatch.sh`) — picks the next eligible phase from   `rbops/phases.json`, runs it, records state, gates it, triggers the next tick. - **Manifest** (`rbops/phases.json`) — the single source of truth. Machine-readable.   If a phase is not in the manifest, it does not exist. - **Gate** (`rbops/verify.sh`) — the only authority on whether work is real. - **Auditor** (`.opencode/agent/auditor.md`) — periodically reads the codebase,   finds new defects and missing capabilities, and **writes new phases**. - **Bootstrap** — the endgame: Redblue compiles Redblue.  ### The state machine  ```   IDLE ──dispatch──▶ RUNNING ──verify ok──▶ REVIEW ──ok──▶ .done ──▶ next phase                       │                       ├─ verify fail ──▶ RETRY (attempt+1)                       ├─ model error ──▶ .deferred (retry later tick)                       ├─ max attempts ──▶ .blocked (needs human)                       └─ budget spent ──▶ .starved (needs new phases) ```  State is **git**, not a database. Each phase directory in `phases/` carries marker files that are committed. Stateless runners therefore work.  ---  ## 1. HARD RULES — violating any of these fails the phase  1. **Do not touch RBOPS itself.** Never edit `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`,    `rbops/phases.json`, or any `.opencode/agent/*.md`. The bot token has no    `workflows` scope and a push touching `.github/` is refused. Do not waste a    phase discovering this. 2. **Never weaken a gate.** You may not modify `verify.sh`, delete a failing    test, add `#[ignore]`, add `.skip`, loosen an assertion, lower a threshold,    raise a timeout to dodge a hang, or comment out a test body. **Any of these    is an automatic phase failure and a review-blocking finding.** There is no    exception for "it was flaky". Flaky tests get fixed, not muted. 3. **No claiming success you did not achieve.** If `verify.sh` is red, the phase    is not done. Report the failure honestly in `REPORT.md` and exit. 4. **Do not run long builds locally.** CI is the only place `cargo build` runs.    Locally use `cargo check --all-targets` at most, and only if essential. 5. **Never commit secrets, API keys, tokens.** Check `.gitignore` covers    `.env`, `*.key`, `target/`. 6. **No destructive git.** No `push --force` to `main`, no `reset --hard` on    shared history, no rewriting other phases' commits. 7. **One phase, one concern.** If you discover work that does not belong to your    phase, do not do it. Record it in `phases/phase-NNN/FINDINGS.md` so the    auditor can promote it to a real phase. 8. **No unbounded speculation.** Do not rewrite the architecture, rename public    types, change the `.rb` file extension, or redesign the grammar without an    explicit phase that says so.  ---  ## 2. Redblue — what must never silently change  Redblue is a tree-walking interpreter: `Lexer → Parser → Analyzer → VM`. Its contract is *readable English-like source*.  **Language invariants.** A phase may not change these without explicitly re-opening the language design in its own PROMPT:  | Invariant | Why | |---|---| | `.rb` is the source extension | Tooling, `rb run`, LSP | | `to … end`, `if … end`, `for … end` use `end`, never braces | Core philosophy | | `set x to <expr>` is assignment | Grammar | | `say` prints | 100% of examples depend on it | | `Value` variants are `Nothing/Number/Text/YesNo/List/Record/Object/Function/Builtin` | Public API, `redblue::Value` is exported | | Errors are `Error::{Lexer,Parser,Analyzer,Runtime,Io}` | Public API | | Trailing-comma and `{interp}` string syntax | Parser tests | | Existing tests in `tests/` keep passing | — |  **Backwards compatibility.** `examples/*.rb` and `modules/*.rb` are the language's specification-by-example. Every phase must keep them working. The gate runs them.  ---  ## 3. Testing mandate — this is where most phases fail  > Every test must assert something that can fail.  Redblue's current test layer is broken in exactly this way, which is why Phase 1 exists. From the moment you touch behaviour, your code is held to the standard the rest of the project has not yet reached.  ### 3.1 Every test must have  1. **A real assertion.** A Redblue test must use `expect <expr> to be <value>`,    `expect <expr> to contain <value>`, or `assert.equal`. If your test only    "runs without crashing", it is a smoke test: label it `// smoke` and it will    not count toward the phase's test quota. 2. **At least one edge case.** The happy path alone is not a test. 3. **A named failure message.** `expect x to be 5` must be able to fail with a    useful message. 4. **Determinism.** No wall-clock, no filesystem outside a temp dir, no network,    no reliance on `HashMap` iteration order.  ### 3.2 Mandatory edge-case matrix  For **every** behaviour you implement or change, test at minimum:  - **Empty / zero / "nothing"** — empty list, empty text, `nothing`, `0`, `""` - **Singleton and boundary** — exactly one element; index `0` and index `len-1` - **Out of bounds** — index `-1`, index `len`, index `999` (must be a clean   runtime error, never a panic, never UB) - **Type mismatch** — number where text expected, record where list expected - **Type coercion boundaries** — `0/0`, `1/0`, `-0.0`, `NaN`, `±Infinity`,   `2^53±1` (precision), `-2^31`, integer overflow past `i64` - **Unicode and escapes** — empty text, `"`, `\`, newline in string,   emoji/CJK/RTL, combining marks, very long strings - **Nesting and recursion** — nested lists, nested records, 3+ closure/object   scopes, mutual recursion - **Duplicate and missing keys** — record with repeated keys, missing field access - **Malformed input** — unterminated string, unclosed `end`, stray token,   empty file, BOM, CRLF line endings, non-UTF8 bytes - **Resource and state** — file that does not exist, permission denied, path with   spaces, deeply nested call stack, infinite loop guard  Pick the rows that apply. A phase that changes the VM must justify in `REPORT.md` which rows are N/A and why.  ### 3.3 Test quota (hard floor, not a target)  The gate enforces a floor: **≥ 3 new `#[test]` functions or ≥ 2 new Redblue `test` blocks**, plus **≥ 1 test named `edge_*`**, **≥ 1 test that asserts a failure is produced**, and **zero** newly-skipped or newly-ignored tests.  Both mandatory tests must be expressible in whichever language you are working in. Write them like this:  | Requirement | Rust | Redblue | |---|---|---| | edge case test | `fn edge_empty_list() { … }` | `test "edge_empty list is rejected" do` | | asserts a failure | `assert!(r.is_err())` / `#[should_panic]` | `try … catch error … end` |  A Redblue test name is a **string literal**, so it is `test "edge_foo"`, with a quote after `test `. Likewise Redblue has no exceptions: `try … catch error` is its failure-catching construct, and a test that faults without one is not asserting anything. Both forms are recognised by the gate.  **Not every phase has a failure channel.** A linter reports problems as a diagnostics collection; a formatter and an LSP emit documents. There is no `Result::Err` to assert on, so the "asserts a failure is produced" rule cannot apply to them. Phases 015, 016 and 017 declare `failure_assert: "not_applicable"` and the gate **skips that one check and says so**. Every other phase keeps it at full strength — a phase that changes what the interpreter *executes* must prove it produces a clean error when it should.  Deliberately, the count floor is low. It exists to catch "no verification at all", not to judge sufficiency — a hard 6 once failed a genuinely good phase with 5 edge tests, which is the gate being smarter than the reviewer. Judging whether the tests COVER the change is the reviewer's job (AGENTS.md section 5: an untested branch is a MAJOR finding, fixed and re-gated). The gate is the floor — cheap, mechanical, never wrong in the pass direction. The reviewer is the ceiling.  ### 3.5 You must implement, not only test  Most rules in this file measure *verification*. None of them measure *ambition*, so the cheapest way to pass the gate would be to write tests for behaviour that already exists and change no implementation at all. That is not a phase.  Every phase in `rbops/phases.json` from 011 onward declares `must_touch` — the areas where that phase's work must land. The gate fails the phase if **no** changed file falls under any of them:  ```json "must_touch": ["src/"]                 // phases 011-013, 015, 016, 018, 019 "must_touch": ["src/", "modules/"]// phase 014, 024 — either is acceptable "must_touch": ["src/", "bootstrap/"]    // phases 021-023 "must_touch": ["tests/"]                // phase 020 — a harness, test-only by design ```  Read your own phase's entry before you start. If your finding genuinely cannot be fixed in the declared area, that is a manifest bug: say so in `FINDINGS.md` with the file:line, and fix it in the area the finding names rather than bending the phase. Phase-024 is the worked example — its finding cites both `src/lexer.rs` and `modules/MathUtils.rb`, so both are declared and either is a valid answer.  Note what this rule is **not**: it is a floor, satisfied by one changed line under `src/`. It says "you implemented something", not "you implemented enough". Judging that the change is correct, complete and proportionate is the reviewer's job, and an `edge_*` test that does not actually pin the new behaviour is a BLOCKER finding.  ### 3.4 The four gates, in order  ``` cargo fmt --check          # formatting        (fails on any diff) cargo clippy -- -D warnings# lints, zero warnings tolerated cargo test                 # full suite, 0 failures ./rbops/verify.sh          # project-specific gates ```  ---  ## 4. REPORT.md contract — required, machine-checked  `phases/phase-NNN/REPORT.md` must exist. `verify.sh` rejects the phase without it.  ```markdown # Phase NNN — <title>  ## What changed | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/vm.rs | +40 −12 | call depth counter in call_builtin |  ## Tests added | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | edge_call_stack_overflow | out of bounds / resource | | test_zero_division | type/numeric boundary |  ## Gates | Gate | Result | |---|---| | cargo fmt --check | pass | | cargo clippy -- -D warnings | pass | | cargo test | 42 passed, 0 failed | | rbops/verify.sh | pass |  ## Invariants touched - None  /  - `expect` now returns a catchable Runtime error (was: process abort)  ## Known gaps / follow-ups - No stack-depth limit for mutually recursive objects yet → FINDINGS.md ```  A REPORT.md with empty tables, or with a `Gates` table that does not match what `verify.sh` printed, is a **fake completion** and will be caught by the auditor.  ---  ## 5. Reviewer contract  The reviewer (`.opencode/agent/reviewer.md`) is **read-only**: no edit, no bash. It reviews the phase diff after the gate is green and emits `FINDINGS:`. Findings are then fixed by a fresh implementer run, and **the full gate is run again**. A phase with open BLOCKER/MAJOR findings is not `.done`.  The reviewer must specifically hunt for:  - **Fake completion** — REPORT.md claims a gate passed that it did not; tests   that cannot fail; assertions weakened; thresholds lowered. - **Panic paths** — any reachable `panic!`/`unwrap`/indexing/`unreachable!`. - **Unbounded resource use** — no recursion limit, no loop guard, no output cap. - **Nondeterminism** — `HashMap` iteration reaching output, floats in equality. - **Error-message regressions** — a previously-rejected program now accepted, or   a crash where an error was expected. - **Spec drift** — behaviour that now contradicts `SPEC.md` or `docs/GRAMMAR.md`.  ---  ## 6. Auditor contract — the phase-generating loop  Every N phases, the **auditor** runs. It must:  1. Measure the codebase: test count, coverage of `src/*.rs`, gate health,    `todo`/`unimplemented`/`FIXME` counts, largest functions, clippy debt. 2. Diff the language's actual behaviour against `SPEC.md` and `ROADMAP.md`    and list every gap as a concrete, file:line-anchored finding. 3. Turn each finding into a **new phase**: id, title, severity, dependencies,    estimated risk, and an acceptance gate — written into `rbops/phases.json`. 4. Refuse to invent work. A phase with no file:line evidence is not created.  The auditor is the only component permitted to append to `phases.json`.  ---  ## 7. Bootstrap contract — the endgame  **Goal:** Redblue compiles Redblue.  This is a ladder, not a leap. Each rung must be green before the next:  | Stage | Artifact | Definition of done | |---|---|---| | **S0** | Rust `rb` (exists today) | `cargo test` green, `rb` runs all `examples/*.rb` | | **S1** | `rb compile` — Redblue **bytecode compiler** written in Rust | emits `.rbc`; `rb vm file.rbc` runs it | | **S2** | The S1 compiler **rewritten in Redblue** | `rb run bootstrap/compiler.rb` produces byte-identical `.rbc` to S1 for a corpus | | **S3** | S2-compiled compiler compiles itself | fixed point: `stage1.rbc == stage2.rbc` byte-for-byte | | **S4** | S3 binary is the shipped `rb` | `rb` built by `rb` passes the full gate |  Non-negotiable rules for any phase in the S2–S4 range:  - **No shimming.** You may not add a Rust-only fast path that the Redblue path   silently falls back to. If it is not implemented in Redblue, it does not exist. - **Fixed-point is the only proof.** "It compiles" is not done. Byte-identical   output over a ≥200-program corpus is done. - **Self-hosting forbids changing the language to make it easier.** Any grammar   change needed for S2+ must be justified by general language merit in its own phase. - **The gate for every bootstrap phase is the S3 fixed-point test**, plus the   normal four gates. No exceptions, no "next phase will finish it".  ---  ## 8. Communication rules  - Report facts, not narrative. "`rbops/verify.sh` reports `edge_foo: FAILED`" beats "there may be an issue with foo". - When blocked, say exactly what you tried and what the exact error was. - Never fabricate a file:line. If you did not read the line, do not cite it. - Prefer the smallest correct change. A 400-line refactor in a bug-fix phase   is a review BLOCKER.  ---  # YOUR PHASE: phase-084  Project root: /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue  # phase-084 — Resolve the reviewer agent's permissions where opencode can actually see them  <!-- GENERATED by rbops/gen-prompts.sh from rbops/phases.json. DO NOT EDIT. -->  | | | |---|---| | Severity | `blocker` | | Risk | `medium` | | Depends on | _none_ | | Timeout | 90 min | | Must change | `tests/` |  ## You must change `tests/`  This phase declares `must_touch: [tests/]`. The gate fails the phase unless at least one changed file falls under it. Writing tests, documentation or a report is not enough — if you finish without touching that area, the phase fails on exactly that check.   ## Evidence — verified finding  ``` `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` is 213 bytes and declares NO `agent` key - `jq -r 'has("agent")'` on it prints false. It sets only `default_agent: "build"` and a permission block whose `edit` and `bash` are both `allow`. The redblue checkout has no opencode.json (`ls redblue/opencode.json` -> No such file or directory). rbops/dispatch.sh:786 invokes opencode as `--dir "$PROJECT_DIR" --agent reviewer`; opencode resolves configuration from the working directory upward and then the global file, so the name `reviewer` is not found there and opencode falls back to `default_agent: build` - a configuration that grants edit and bash. The reviewer is contractually read-only: rbops/agents/reviewer.md opens 'You are READ-ONLY: you cannot edit files or run commands', and the pipeline's own opencode.json sets `agent.reviewer.permission` to `edit: deny, bash: deny`. NEITHER is in the resolution path. dispatch.sh:727-732 already acknowledges the resolution failure in prose and inlines the CONTRACT, which is why every phase has had one - but the PERMISSIONS remain unenforced, so the single component whose job is to catch a compromised phase is the one component that is allowed to edit it, and nothing in the gate would notice if it did. ```  **Re-verify this before you change anything.** If the finding no longer reproduces on `main`, stop and write that to `FINDINGS.md` — a stale phase must never be "fixed" by inventing a change.  ## Goal  Ship the agent definitions inside the checkout opencode resolves, so `--agent reviewer` and `--agent verifier` carry their declared permissions instead of inheriting the global build agent's.  ## Method  1. Read `AGENTS.md` in full. Obey every hard rule; they are not negotiable. 2. Reproduce the finding. Record the exact command and the exact wrong output. 3. Write the failing test FIRST. Watch it fail. Do not touch production code    until you have a red test that fails for the right reason. 4. Make the smallest change that turns it green. 5. Run the four gates yourself before claiming anything:     ```bash    cargo fmt --all -- --check    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    cargo test --all-targets    ./rbops/verify.sh phase-084    ```  6. Write `REPORT.md` in the exact shape required by `AGENTS.md` section 4.  ## Definition of done  - [ ] the project ships an opencode.json whose `agent.reviewer.permission` denies BOTH edit and bash, and whose `agent.verifier.permission` allows both - [ ] a test reads that file and asserts all four permission values, so removing a single deny fails `cargo test` - [ ] a test asserts the file declares no `default_agent`, so the implementer agent cannot be quietly repointed - [ ] the file lives at the repository root and NOT under `.opencode/agent/`, which rbops/verify.sh:127 treats as a forbidden path - [ ] REPORT.md records the opencode configuration-resolution rule this relies on (working directory first, then the global file) with a citation, and says plainly that the test can only assert the file's contents, not opencode's resolution - [ ] edge_* tests cover: the file missing, the `agent` key missing, `reviewer` missing, `verifier` missing, and one permission flipped to allow - five distinct failing cases - [ ] `cargo test` reports 0 failures and `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean with no `allow(` added  ## Test requirements — non-negotiable  From `AGENTS.md` section 3. Tick each row that applies to this change. For every row you do NOT cover, write one line in `REPORT.md` saying why.  - [ ] empty — covered / N/A + why - [ ] singleton — covered / N/A + why - [ ] boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] out_of_bounds — covered / N/A + why - [ ] type_mismatch — covered / N/A + why - [ ] numeric_boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] unicode — covered / N/A + why - [ ] nesting_recursion — covered / N/A + why - [ ] duplicate_missing_keys — covered / N/A + why - [ ] malformed_input — covered / N/A + why - [ ] resource_limit — covered / N/A + why  You must also:  - at least 3 new `#[test]` functions (or at least 2 new Redblue `test` blocks).   This is a floor, not a target: it catches "no verification at all". Whether   your tests COVER the change is judged by the reviewer, and an untested branch   is a MAJOR finding that sends the phase back. - at least 1 test named `edge_*` - at least 1 test that asserts a **failure** is produced, not just a success - zero new `#[ignore]`, `// skip`, or `allow(clippy::` suppressions - zero newly-failing pre-existing tests  ## Constraints  - Do not touch `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/`, or   `rbops/phases.json`. The gate rejects these paths, and the bot token has no   `workflows` scope so a push touching `.github/` is refused by GitHub. - Do not weaken, delete or re-scope any existing test. - Do not change the language surface listed in `phases/INVARIANTS.md`. - Keep the diff on-topic. A refactor smuggled into a bug-fix phase is a   review BLOCKER. - Do not update README or ROADMAP to claim this works. Update them only when   the gate is green and you have run the examples yourself.  ## If you are blocked  Do not fake progress. Write what you tried, the exact error, and what you believe is required into `FINDINGS.md`, then stop.  A blocked phase with an honest report is worth more than a green phase with a lie in it. `verify.sh` will reject the phase anyway; a fabrication only adds a blocking review finding on top.  --- ## CONTINUATION MODE A previous attempt was interrupted and its partial work is already in the tree. DO NOT restart. Inspect what exists, continue from it, finish the phase.  --- ## RESUMED WORK — read this before touching anything A previous attempt's work is already merged into your tree. DO NOT start over and DO NOT rewrite it from scratch. Your job is to finish what is missing:  1. Run the four gates FIRST, before changing anything, to see the current state. 2. Read phases/phase-084/REPORT.md from the previous attempt (harvested below if    present) to see what it claimed and what the gate actually said. 3. Fix exactly what failed. Add what is missing. Do not remove working code.  ### Previous REPORT.md (do not trust its gate claims — verify them)  ``` # Phase 084 — Resolve the reviewer agent's permissions where opencode can actually see them  ## Result  `opencode.json` sits at the repository root of this checkout and declares the pipeline's two agents: `reviewer`, denied `edit` and `bash` (and every other key that could widen it), and `verifier`, allowed both. opencode resolves that file before `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`, so `--agent reviewer` binds to a read-only agent instead of falling through to the global `default_agent: build`.  Round 9 closed the last way the guarantee could be **true in the working tree and false in the checkout the pipeline actually clones**. Every test the phase had inherited read `opencode.json` out of the working tree. That file can be present, correct, and still not be in the repository: `git rm --cached opencode.json` leaves it exactly where it was, every assertion in the suite still passes, and the pipeline — which clones this repository — finds no project config at all, so `--agent reviewer` resolves to the global build agent again, whose `edit` and `bash` are `allow`. The reviewer is the one component whose writability no gate notices, because nothing a gate runs asks the reviewer to write something.  Five tests close that, and one more closes a second reach: a top-level key in the file applies to **every** agent in the project, not only the two it names, so the file now carries an allow-list of top-level keys and refuses `instructions`, `model`, `mcp`, `plugin` and the rest. `instructions` is the sharp one — it is prepended to the system prompt of every agent, so a line there is a line the reviewer reads as an instruction from the pipeline rather than from its own contract.  **Round 10 closed the same reach one level down, and one hole in the reviewer's own block.** The top-level allow-list stops at the top level: the checks that reached an agent looked at `mode` and `permission` and at nothing else, so every other key of `agent.reviewer` was accepted silently — and the published schema's `AgentConfig` carries a `prompt` key, which is the agent's OWN prompt. A line there reaches the reviewer exactly as directly as a top-level `instructions` array does, one level down. The agent blocks are now allow-listed too (`description`, `mode`, `permission` and nothing else), and `prompt`, `tools`, `model`, `options` and the rest of the schema's keys are refused.  The reviewer's permission block had a hole of its own, in the direction that matters: the allow-list it was checked against permitted `read`, `glob`, `grep` and `list` to be granted, so `read: "allow"` passed — while this document has called that a **widening** since round 2, because opencode's default `read` rules ask about `.env` files (`read *.env ask`) and an agent-level `read: "allow"` is appended after them and wins the last-match. A future edit that added it would have re-allowed `.env` reads and the whole suite would have stayed green. The reviewer's block is now required to declare **exactly** the eleven keys it must deny, and the four read-only keys are refused outright, in either direction.  **Round 11 closed the last open list in the file.** Every level of it was closed but one: the reviewer's permission block is exactly the eleven keys it must deny, the agent blocks are exactly `description`, `mode` and `permission`, and the top level is exactly `$schema` and `agent` — but the **verifier's** permission block was still open, in both directions. A third key there was accepted silently: a grant no assertion in this suite reads (`external_directory`, `read`, `task`), a misspelling (`bashh`) that is dead on arrival while the key it meant stays undeclared, and a **denial** — which is the sharp one, because the verifier runs the gates and the gates need the toolchain, and the toolchain is outside the checkout: cargo reads `~/.cargo` and rustup keeps the compilers in `~/.rustup`. An `external_directory: "deny"` there would stop cargo reading its own registry, which is a way to break the gate that changes nothing the reviewer can see. The verifier's block is now required to declare exactly the two tools it needs — `edit` and `bash`, both allowed — and nothing else.  **This round closed the last way the phase's own audit could be red.** The acknowledgement suite audits every commit in `HEAD`'s ancestry whose subject marks unreviewed work against a written list — and it pinned the delivery commit's **SHA**. That commit is created by the pipeline *after* the run that wrote the work, and re-created on every round, squashed onto the same parent, so the SHA the previous round pinned leaves `HEAD`'s ancestry the moment the next one lands. The tree this delivery commits therefore failed the phase's own audit test the moment the pipeline created the commit: one commit reported as unreviewed work, and one entry reported as stale. No SHA can be right here — the one that is right today is wrong the moment the next round lands, and the one that is right tomorrow does not exist yet. The delivery commit is now acknowledged by its **subject**, which is the part that survives the rebuild, and its SHA is resolved out of the log at audit time: a rebuilt delivery commit is neither unreviewed nor stale **by construction**. Three tests came with it, and one of them runs the audit against a clone rebuilt the way the pipeline rebuilds it — the measurement that settles the finding rather than arguing about it.  Two smaller findings came with it. The report's own diff tables did not match the diff being shipped, and one of them was not arithmetic but a category error; both are re-derived from `git diff --numstat` now, and the report says which of its numbers is self-referential. And the permission-key completeness test asserted a partition of three constants written in the same file under a name that promised more than it could deliver: it is renamed to what it asserts, and the claim it makes is the one it can make.  **This round found the phase's own audit failing on the first run in a fresh checkout, and fixed the race that caused it.** The gates were run first, as the continuation instructions require, and the fourth of them was red — not the gate, the suite, in the file this phase has owned since round 11. Two tests failed with `this history must be complete before it is measured: "history is shallow and completing the clone did not make it complete"`, and one with `HEAD's history must be readable and acknowledged` and the same reason.  The clone was shallow because the pipeline clones the project with a depth, and the same run's fetch had already completed it — `.git/shallow` was gone and the history was whole by the time the failure printed. Two tests had read the depth **before** that fetch landed, run a fetch of their own, and read the depth again **before** the first fetch finished. `cargo test` runs the tests in a binary on parallel threads, and both of those tests complete the clone before they audit it, so on a shallow clone **both** ran `git fetch --unshallow` at the same instant. The loser of that race is told by git what happened, and the audit — which exists to refuse a truncated log — refused a clone that was complete a moment later:  ``` thread B: depth before fetch = true thread B fetch: fatal: Unable to create '.../.git/shallow.lock': File exists. thread B: depth after fetch  = true thread A: depth after fetch  = false ```  This is not a new shape, and this repository already knows it: `tests/checkpoint_ancestry_test.rs` says in its own words that "on a shallow clone all three would run `git fetch --unshallow` at the same instant and collide on `.git/shallow.lock`", and serialises the repair behind a `Mutex` for that reason. The acknowledgement suite had the same helper, the same two callers, and no such lock, so it carried the race the other file had already paid for once. **The repair is now serialised the same way**, the decision about it is split out of the subprocess call so it can be driven from fixtures, and a repair that lands late is accepted instead of being reported as a clone that cannot be completed — with a bounded number of attempts, so a clone that really cannot be completed is still refused.  **This round is the review of that round, and the review was right about it.** Four MAJOR findings, two MINOR and one STYLE, and the two that mattered most were about *measurement*, not about code: the bounded retry did not remove the failure it was written for, and the round's own "after" measurement never exercised the contended path at all. Three attempts a second apart is a **window**, not a guarantee — a fetch that loses `.git/shallow.lock` to a fetch that is still running leaves the clone exactly as short as it was, so every attempt made while that fetch runs loses too, and the clone becomes complete a moment after the refusal. Round 13 measured a checkout with no competing fetch in flight, where the first attempt succeeded and the retry was never taken.  So the repair now waits for the clone's **state** rather than for a count of attempts: the depth is re-read after every attempt, and between attempts — and after the attempts are spent — the depth is watched until the fetch that holds the lock lands. Both are bounded (`RepairPolicy::attempts`, `RepairPolicy::wait`), so a clone that really cannot be completed is still refused, and refused with the reason it refused. The serialisation test now drives the **repair path itself**, and the peak concurrency is measured by a counter incremented inside that path's locked region — round 13's version took the mutex itself and proved only that a mutex excludes, so deleting the lock from `ensure_full_history` left the suite green. The sibling audit file this phase has cited as the model had the identical lost-lock refusal and now has the same repair.  The counterfactual was measured with a competing fetch in flight, holding `.git/shallow.lock` for twelve seconds — longer than the whole attempt window — on a shallow copy of this checkout:  | | round 13's repair (three attempts, no state watch) | this round | |---|---|---| | `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` | **21 of 23 green** — the audit refused the history with `fatal: Unable to create '.../.git/shallow.lock': File exists.` | **23 of 23 green** | | `tests/checkpoint_ancestry_test.rs` | refused the same way, with no retry at all | **25 of 25 green** | | the clone the audit measured | complete (162 commits) a few seconds *after* the refusal | complete (162 commits) when the audit measured it |  **The gates.** The full suite is green: **1963 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored**, across the repository's test binaries, re-measured in this round. `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` are clean, and `rbops/verify.sh phase-084` passes. No gate was weakened, no test was deleted, no assertion was loosened, no threshold was moved, and no `#[ignore]`, `// skip` or `allow(clippy::` was added.  **What these tests cannot prove.** They read a file and assert its contents and its shipping state. They do not run opencode, so they cannot prove which file opencode opened, or that a given opencode build resolves the name `reviewer` to this definition. A future opencode that changed its resolution order would leave this suite green while the reviewer again ran with edit access. What the suite does prove is that the file says the right thing, that it is in the repository, and that it fails the moment a `deny` is dropped, an agent is renamed, a mode is changed, a third tool is granted to the reviewer or the verifier, a `default_agent` is reintroduced, an agent this file does not own appears in it, a top-level `permission` block or any other top-level key is added to it, a key the file does not own is added to either agent block, a read-only permission is declared for the reviewer, or the file stops shipping.  The tests this round added make a narrower claim still: they drive the **decision** about the history repair from fixtures and assert that the repair is one at a time, that a repair that lands late is accepted — including one that lands *after every attempt has been spent*, which is the case a count of attempts cannot survive — that a complete history is never repaired, that a clone that cannot be completed is refused with the reason, and that the clone this suite is auditing is complete when the audit starts. They do not prove that the pipeline's checkout is shallow or not, that the origin is reachable, or that no other process is fetching the same clone at the same moment; the last of those is **absorbed by the repair waiting for the clone's state**, not excluded, and it was measured that way — with a competing fetch holding `.git/shallow.lock` for twelve seconds.  **Where the history of this phase lives.** Rounds 1–12 wrote their accounts into earlier revisions of this document and into `phases/phase-084/FINDINGS.md`, which keeps the running audit section by section, each tagged with the round that wrote it. This document describes the delivery that is in the tree now; the earlier rounds are in this file's git history and in FINDINGS.md.  ## What changed  The delivery as a whole, measured against the base the dispatcher captured before the resume merge (`b21821d`):  | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | `opencode.json` | +30 −0 | The shipped project config: the reviewer's eleven denies, the verifier's two allows, no `default_agent`, no top-level `permission` block. Unchanged since round 8 — `git diff --stat -- opencode.json` is empty. | | `tests/agent_permissions_test.rs` | +1995 −0 | The whole suite: the permission contract, the mode, the shipping state of the file, the top-level allow-list, the agent-level allow-list, the reviewer's exact-key rule and the `git` failure message (round 10), the verifier's exact-key rule (round 11), and the permission-key partition test, renamed to what it asserts (round 12). | | `tests/report_markdown_test.rs` | +453 −0 | The phase's own two delivery documents must read the same way under every markdown reader, and the report must still carry the four sections the gate and the preflight look for. | | `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` | +929 −19 | The acknowledgement data, the delivery-commit rule that replaced the SHA-pinned entry the pipeline's per-round rebuild made stale (round 12), the serialised history repair that stopped the audit failing on t